roposition existence_uniqueness_prop, P1(ii) (around 200\u2013213) requires \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\Psi+\\\\\\\\Psi\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top\\\\\\\\preceq-\\\\\\\\upsilon I\\\\\\\\) with \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\upsilon>0\\\\\\\\), and its price-price block in row \\\\\\\\(n,m\\\\\\\\) is \\\\\\\\(-A_{nm}\\\"'^n'\\\"\\\\\\\\). Set a nonzero direction \\\\\\\\(v\\\\\\\\) with every allocation component zero and every price component the same \\\\\\\\(h\\\\\\\\). Then \\\\\\\\(v\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top\\\\\\\\Psi v=-\\\\\\\\sum_n h\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_m A_{nm}\\\"'^n)h=0'\\\"\\\\\\\\), hence \\\\\\\\(v\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\Psi+\\\\\\\\Psi\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top)v=0\\\\\\\\), contradicting strict negativity. Thus no tax can satisfy Q\u2019s stated P1 and P2 LMIs jointly, including Eq. (40); Q Proposition prop2 is false as stated. This does not by itself disprove equilibrium uniqueness via another argument. It blocks invoking Q\u2019s strong-monotonicity certificate for the proposed CVaR hybrid. I am checking whether a narrower value-stability result survives without that certificate.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_24\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind objection --text 'Major correction to my #4: Q\u2019s advertised LMI certificate is algebraically infeasible. Q Theorem nash_implementation_theorem, P2(i) (inputs/Q.tex around 240\u2013250) requires \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_m A_{nm}\\\"'^n=0'\\\"\\\\\\\\) for every \\\\\\\\(n\\\\\\\\). Q Proposition existence_uniqueness_prop, P1(ii) (around 200\u2013213) requires \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\Psi+\\\\\\\\Psi\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top\\\\\\\\preceq-\\\\\\\\upsilon I\\\\\\\\) with \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\upsilon>0\\\\\\\\), and its price-price block in row \\\\\\\\(n,m\\\\\\\\) is \\\\\\\\(-A_{nm}\\\"'^n'\\\"\\\\\\\\). Set a nonzero direction \\\\\\\\(v\\\\\\\\) with every allocation component zero and every price component the same \\\\\\\\(h\\\\\\\\). Then \\\\\\\\(v\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top\\\\\\\\Psi v=-\\\\\\\\sum_n h\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_m A_{nm}\\\"'^n)h=0'\\\"\\\\\\\\), hence \\\\\\\\(v\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\Psi+\\\\\\\\Psi\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top)v=0\\\\\\\\), contradicting strict negativity. Thus no tax can satisfy Q\u2019s stated P1 and P2 LMIs jointly, including Eq. (40); Q Proposition prop2 is false as stated. This does not by itself disprove equilibrium uniqueness via another argument. It blocks invoking Q\u2019s strong-monotonicity certificate for the proposed CVaR hybrid. I am checking whether a narrower value-stability result survives without that certificate.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"5\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_25\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"A concrete obstruction changes the direction of the comparison: Q\u2019s strict uniqueness LMI conflicts with one of its own implementation conditions. I\u2019ve posted the short algebraic contradiction to the ledger. I\u2019m now separating what that invalidates from what may still be proved about a CVaR based mechanism.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_26\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I\u2019m putting the longer derivation in `ada/` so the ledger can stay concise. I\u2019m applying the style-gates skill to check the note before I share it.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_27\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_27\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: style-gates\\ndescription: >-\\n  Run and enforce lightweight style gates for Markdown, LaTeX, and PDF artifacts before Codex\\n  shares, commits, pushes, or produces .md, .tex, or .pdf files. Use when writing, editing,\\n  reviewing, generating, or pushing reports, papers, slides, documentation, markdown notes,\\n  TeX sources, or PDFs that should pass basic style and integrity checks.\\n---\\n\\n# Style Gates\\n\\n## Core Rule\\n\\nBefore finalizing, sharing, committing, or pushing any `.md`, `.tex`, or `.pdf` artifact,\\nidentify the affected files and run the gate. Fix failures before delivery. If a failure is\\nintentionally left unresolved, say exactly which gate failed and why.\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nFrom the repository root, run:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed\\n```\\n\\nBefore commit or push, prefer:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --staged\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed --base origin/main\\n```\\n\\nFor a specific artifact:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py report.md paper.tex slides.pdf\\n```\\n\\nUse `--strict-warnings` when the recipient wants warnings to block delivery.\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n1. Determine scope:\\n   - For generated files, pass the exact output paths.\\n   - For a commit, use `--staged`.\\n   - For a branch or push, use `--changed --base origin/main` or the relevant upstream base.\\n   - For an initial audit, use `--all`.\\n2. Run the gate script.\\n3. Fix all errors. Treat warnings as review prompts unless `--strict-warnings` is required.\\n4. For LaTeX and PDFs, also use project-native validation when available:\\n   - Compile `.tex` with the repo's existing command or `latexmk`/`pdflatex`.\\n   - Open or inspect generated PDFs enough to confirm they are not blank, corrupt, or stale.\\n5. Re-run the gate after edits.\\n\\n## Human Review Gates\\n\\nThe script catches mechanical issues; still review these before sharing.\\n\\nMarkdown:\\n- Clear title and section hierarchy.\\n- No unresolved placeholders, merge markers, stale TODOs, or fake links.\\n- Tables, lists, math, and fenced code render plausibly.\\n- Claims with external facts have sources or are clearly marked as assumptions.\\n\\nLaTeX:\\n- The file compiles, or the reason it was not compiled is reported.\\n- No unresolved citations/references in the build log when a PDF is produced.\\n- No obvious placeholder text, broken environments, or empty `\\\\ref{}` / `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n- Generated PDFs correspond to the current `.tex`, not an older build artifact.\\n\\nPDF:\\n- File opens, has at least one page, and is not zero-byte/corrupt.\\n- Expected text is visible/extractable unless the PDF is intentionally image-only.\\n- No accidental blank pages, cropped content, or stale draft/watermark text.\\n\\n## Integration\\n\\nFor repo hooks, CI, and copy/paste setup, read `references/repo-integration.md`.\\nTemplates live in `assets/`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 5'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 5'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#6 [emmy/objection] Critical check on Q: its proposed payment (Eq. (40)) cannot satisfy its own strict P1 LMI (Eq. (existence_uniqueness_cond_eq)), despite Proposition (prop2). For any \\\\(N\\\\ge2\\\\), its price-dependent own-price derivative is \\\\(\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-\\\\alpha[p_n-\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1)]+\\\\alpha(\\\\sum_m x_m-c)/(N-1)\\\\). Holding \\\\(x\\\\) fixed and moving all prices by the same vector \\\\(h\\\\) leaves this pseudogradient component unchanged. Thus the price-price Jacobian has common-price null vector \\\\((h,\\\\ldots,h)\\\\), so the full symmetric pseudogradient Jacobian has zero quadratic form on \\\\((\\\\Delta x=0,\\\\Delta p=(h,\\\\ldots,h))\\\\); it cannot be \\\\(\\\\preceq-\\\\upsilon I\\\\) for \\\\(\\\\upsilon>0\\\\). This blocks using Q\u2019s claimed explicit LMI certificate as a theorem. It does not by itself prove nonunique NE; a different uniqueness proof or redesigned tax is needed. P\u2019s CVaR oracle still provides a separate route to quantitative approximate-equilibrium guarantees.\\nlatest substantive entry: 6\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_29\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind finding --text 'Beyond the LMI contradiction in #5/#6, Eq. (40) can have genuinely nonunique Nash equilibria. Take \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,c=2,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_1=\\\\\\\\mathcal X_2=[0,1],\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(V_n(x)=2x-x\\\"'^2/2'\\\"\\\\\\\\). For each \\\\\\\\(p\\\\\\\\in[0,1]\\\\\\\\), the profile \\\\\\\\((x_1,p_1,x_2,p_2)=(1,p,1,p)\\\\\\\\) is NE. Directly from Q Eq. (40), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_n}U_n=V_n\\\\\\\\prime(x_n)+p_n-2p_{-n}\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-p_n+p_{-n}+x_1+x_2-c\\\\\\\\), and the own-strategy Hessian is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-1&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\1&-1\\\\\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\\\\\preceq0\\\\\\\\). At the profile, these derivatives are \\\\\\\\(1-p\\\\\\\\ge0\\\\\\\\) at upper bound \\\\\\\\(x_n=1\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\) for price, proving a global best response. Thus Q\u2019s claimed unique NE fails under its stated compact, strongly concave valuation assumptions; optimum allocation remains unique. This is compatible with P\u2019s genuinely stochastic risk model: let \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\xi=\\\\\\\\pm 1/4\\\\\\\\) equiprobably, \\\\\\\\(J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x\\\"'^2/2-(9/4+'\\\"\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), and take tail level \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\beta=1/2\\\\\\\\); then \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{1/2}J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x\\\"'^2/2-2x'\\\"\\\\\\\\) on \\\\\\\\([0,1]\\\\\\\\). P\u2019s bounded convex Lipschitz sample-loss assumptions hold on this compact set. I will put the derivation in ada/.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_29\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind finding --text 'Beyond the LMI contradiction in #5/#6, Eq. (40) can have genuinely nonunique Nash equilibria. Take \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,c=2,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_1=\\\\\\\\mathcal X_2=[0,1],\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(V_n(x)=2x-x\\\"'^2/2'\\\"\\\\\\\\). For each \\\\\\\\(p\\\\\\\\in[0,1]\\\\\\\\), the profile \\\\\\\\((x_1,p_1,x_2,p_2)=(1,p,1,p)\\\\\\\\) is NE. Directly from Q Eq. (40), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_n}U_n=V_n\\\\\\\\prime(x_n)+p_n-2p_{-n}\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-p_n+p_{-n}+x_1+x_2-c\\\\\\\\), and the own-strategy Hessian is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-1&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\1&-1\\\\\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\\\\\preceq0\\\\\\\\). At the profile, these derivatives are \\\\\\\\(1-p\\\\\\\\ge0\\\\\\\\) at upper bound \\\\\\\\(x_n=1\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\) for price, proving a global best response. Thus Q\u2019s claimed unique NE fails under its stated compact, strongly concave valuation assumptions; optimum allocation remains unique. This is compatible with P\u2019s genuinely stochastic risk model: let \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\xi=\\\\\\\\pm 1/4\\\\\\\\) equiprobably, \\\\\\\\(J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x\\\"'^2/2-(9/4+'\\\"\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), and take tail level \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\beta=1/2\\\\\\\\); then \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{1/2}J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x\\\"'^2/2-2x'\\\"\\\\\\\\) on \\\\\\\\([0,1]\\\\\\\\). P\u2019s bounded convex Lipschitz sample-loss assumptions hold on this compact set. I will put the derivation in ada/.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"7\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_30\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 7'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_30\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 7'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#8 [emmy/finding] Despite #5\u20136, Q Eq. (40) appears to admit a direct full-implementation proof without strict monotonicity. For each resource \\\\(k\\\\), write \\\\(P^k=\\\\sum_n p_n^k\\\\) and \\\\(z^k=\\\\sum_n x_n^k-c^k\\\\). Q\u2019s tax gives \\\\(\\\\partial_{p_n^k}U_n=-\\\\alpha(Np_n^k-P^k-z^k)/(N-1)\\\\). At a price best response, if any \\\\(p_n^k>0\\\\), then \\\\(Np_n^k=P^k+z^k>0\\\\), forcing every price positive/equal and then \\\\(z^k=0\\\\) by summation. If all prices vanish, the boundary derivative condition gives \\\\(z^k\\\\le0\\\\). Hence every NE has common \\\\(p^k\\\\ge0\\\\), feasible allocations, and \\\\(p^k z^k=0\\\\). At common prices, \\\\(\\\\partial_{x_n}t_n=\\\\alpha p\\\\), so each allocation best response maximizes \\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n\\\\). These are precisely planner KKT conditions with \\\\(\\\\lambda=\\\\alpha p\\\\), proving every NE allocation optimal for concave \\\\(V_n\\\\), even nonsmooth via subgradients. Conversely a planner primal-dual pair yields an NE (assuming a multiplier exists). Q\u2019s unique-message claim fails: \\\\(N=2,K=1,\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],c=2,V_n(x)=2x-x^2/2,\\\\alpha=1\\\\) has \\\\(x_1=x_2=1\\\\) and any common \\\\(p\\\\in[0,1]\\\\) as NE. This outcome-only theorem is the safer foundation for combining Q with P\u2019s CVaR oracle.\\nlatest substantive entry: 8\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_31\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 8'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_31\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 8'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#9 [emmy/correction] Qualification to #8: the converse from planner primal-dual pair to a Nash equilibrium needs Q\u2019s \\\\(\\\\alpha\\\\)-strong concavity (or another condition making each player\u2019s objective jointly concave in \\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\)), not mere concavity. In Eq. (40), the own \\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\) cross coefficient is \\\\(\\\\alpha/(N-1)\\\\), while the own price curvature is \\\\(-\\\\alpha\\\\). Combining Q\u2019s \\\\(-\\\\alpha\\\\) curvature in \\\\(x_n\\\\) gives a negative semidefinite own Hessian for \\\\(N\\\\ge2\\\\); then the first-order conditions suffice. The forward statement, every existing NE allocates optimally, only needs concavity of the planner valuation and the price best-response identities.\\nlatest substantive entry: 9\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_32\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind finding --text 'Further correction: Q Eq. (40) also fails its individual-rationality claim under stated assumptions. Let \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,c=1,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_1(x)=4x-x\\\"'^2/2'\\\"\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_2(x)=x-x\\\"'^2/2'\\\"\\\\\\\\). The social optimum is \\\\\\\\((x_1,x_2)=(1,0)\\\\\\\\). At common price \\\\\\\\(p_1=p_2=3\\\\\\\\), all price derivatives vanish; \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_1}U_1=V_1\\\\\\\\prime(1)-3=0\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_2}U_2=V_2\\\\\\\\prime(0)-3=-2\\\\\\\\le0\\\\\\\\), and own utilities are jointly concave, so this is an NE. Substitution into Q Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_1=3p(x_1-c/2)=9/2\\\\\\\\), whereas \\\\\\\\(V_1(1)=7/2\\\\\\\\); hence \\\\\\\\(U_1=-1<0=V_1(0)\\\\\\\\). The IR proof\u2019s proposed zero-demand deviation also fails: holding prices and rival fixed, \\\\\\\\(t_1((0,3),(0,3))=3/2>0\\\\\\\\). This can arise from P-compatible CVaR losses \\\\\\\\(J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x\\\"'^2/2-(b_n+1/4+'\\\"\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(b_1=4,b_2=1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\xi=\\\\\\\\pm1/4\\\\\\\\) equiprobably, tail \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\beta=1/2\\\\\\\\). Thus my #4 candidate theorem must drop exact/approximate IR for Eq. (40), unless a redesigned transfer rule is proved. Exact budget balance and optimal allocation still survive. I will document this counterexample in ada/.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_32\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind finding --text 'Further correction: Q Eq. (40) also fails its individual-rationality claim under stated assumptions. Let \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,c=1,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_1(x)=4x-x\\\"'^2/2'\\\"\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_2(x)=x-x\\\"'^2/2'\\\"\\\\\\\\). The social optimum is \\\\\\\\((x_1,x_2)=(1,0)\\\\\\\\). At common price \\\\\\\\(p_1=p_2=3\\\\\\\\), all price derivatives vanish; \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_1}U_1=V_1\\\\\\\\prime(1)-3=0\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_2}U_2=V_2\\\\\\\\prime(0)-3=-2\\\\\\\\le0\\\\\\\\), and own utilities are jointly concave, so this is an NE. Substitution into Q Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_1=3p(x_1-c/2)=9/2\\\\\\\\), whereas \\\\\\\\(V_1(1)=7/2\\\\\\\\); hence \\\\\\\\(U_1=-1<0=V_1(0)\\\\\\\\). The IR proof\u2019s proposed zero-demand deviation also fails: holding prices and rival fixed, \\\\\\\\(t_1((0,3),(0,3))=3/2>0\\\\\\\\). This can arise from P-compatible CVaR losses \\\\\\\\(J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x\\\"'^2/2-(b_n+1/4+'\\\"\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(b_1=4,b_2=1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\xi=\\\\\\\\pm1/4\\\\\\\\) equiprobably, tail \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\beta=1/2\\\\\\\\). Thus my #4 candidate theorem must drop exact/approximate IR for Eq. (40), unless a redesigned transfer rule is proved. Exact budget balance and optimal allocation still survive. I will document this counterexample in ada/.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"10\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_33\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The obstruction is stronger than an invalid proof: Q\u2019s published tax admits multiple equilibrium prices, and I found an equilibrium where an agent\u2019s utility is below its outside option. I also found a transfer adjustment that depends only on other agents\u2019 messages, so it preserves best responses while restoring the intended equilibrium payment and individual rationality. I\u2019m checking that algebra before recording it.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_34\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 10'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_34\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 10'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#11 [emmy/objection] Further failure of Q Eq. (40): individual rationality can fail at a genuine NE. Let \\\\(N=2,K=1,\\\\mathcal X_1=\\\\mathcal X_2=[0,1],c=1,\\\\alpha=0.1\\\\), \\\\(V_1(x)=1.1x-0.05x^2\\\\), \\\\(V_2(x)=0.5x-0.05x^2\\\\). The planner optimum is \\\\((1,0)\\\\), with multiplier \\\\(\\\\lambda=1\\\\); Eq. (40) has an NE at \\\\((x_1,x_2,p_1,p_2)=(1,0,10,10)\\\\). Indeed the own payoff Hessian is \\\\(\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-0.1&0.1\\\\\\\\0.1&-0.1\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\preceq0\\\\), and at this profile price derivatives vanish, agent 1\u2019s allocation derivative is \\\\(V_1\\\\prime(1)-1=0\\\\), agent 2\u2019s is \\\\(V_2\\\\prime(0)-1=-0.5\\\\) at lower bound. Direct substitution in Eq. (40) gives \\\\(t_1=1.5\\\\), \\\\(t_2=-1.5\\\\). Thus \\\\(U_1=V_1(1)-t_1=1.05-1.5=-0.45<0=V_1(0)\\\\), contradicting Q\u2019s P4/Proposition (prop2). P-compatible stochastic losses realizing these valuations are \\\\(J_1(x,\\\\xi)=0.05x^2-(1.2+\\\\xi)x\\\\), \\\\(J_2(x,\\\\xi)=0.05x^2-(0.6+\\\\xi)x\\\\), with \\\\(\\\\xi=\\\\pm0.1\\\\) equiprobably and CVaR upper-tail level \\\\(\\\\beta=1/2\\\\); then \\\\(-\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{1/2}J_n=V_n\\\\) on \\\\([0,1]\\\\), and each sample loss is convex, bounded, Lipschitz. A Q\u2013P synthesis should target efficient allocations and budget balance first, then redesign transfers if exact IR is required.\\nlatest substantive entry: 11\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_35\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind idea --text 'Proposed exact repair of Q Eq. (40) IR, without changing any best response: write \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=(N\\\"'^2-N+1)/(N-1)''^2=1+N/(N-1)''^2'\\\"\\\\\\\\). Direct substitution at any common-price complementary NE gives \\\\\\\\(t_n=\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\kappa_N p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\) (terms involving \\\\\\\\(p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c)\\\\\\\\) vanish). Add the others-only rebate \\\\\\\\(R_n(s_{-n})=\\\\\\\\alpha(\\\\\\\\kappa_N-1)(\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1))\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\bar x_{-n}-(N-1)c/N)\\\\\\\\), and define \\\\\\\\(t_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=t_n+R_n\\\\\\\\). At NE, \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1)=p\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\bar x_{-n}=c-x_n\\\\\\\\) in every priced coordinate, so \\\\\\\\(t_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\). Hence \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_n t_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=0\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(U_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}\\\\\\\\ge V_n(0)+\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top c/N\\\\\\\\ge0\\\\\\\\), since \\\\\\\\(x_n\\\\\\\\) maximizes \\\\\\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top x_n\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\\\\\). The rebate uses no \\\\\\\\(s_n\\\\\\\\), so NE set and efficient allocation are unchanged. Need check the formula carefully for slack coordinates and transfer signs; this could be the constructive Q\u2013P line once paired with P\u2019s CVaR surrogate oracle.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_35\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind idea --text 'Proposed exact repair of Q Eq. (40) IR, without changing any best response: write \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=(N\\\"'^2-N+1)/(N-1)''^2=1+N/(N-1)''^2'\\\"\\\\\\\\). Direct substitution at any common-price complementary NE gives \\\\\\\\(t_n=\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\kappa_N p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\) (terms involving \\\\\\\\(p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c)\\\\\\\\) vanish). Add the others-only rebate \\\\\\\\(R_n(s_{-n})=\\\\\\\\alpha(\\\\\\\\kappa_N-1)(\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1))\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\bar x_{-n}-(N-1)c/N)\\\\\\\\), and define \\\\\\\\(t_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=t_n+R_n\\\\\\\\). At NE, \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1)=p\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\bar x_{-n}=c-x_n\\\\\\\\) in every priced coordinate, so \\\\\\\\(t_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\). Hence \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_n t_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=0\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(U_n\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}\\\\\\\\ge V_n(0)+\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top c/N\\\\\\\\ge0\\\\\\\\), since \\\\\\\\(x_n\\\\\\\\) maximizes \\\\\\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top x_n\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\\\\\). The rebate uses no \\\\\\\\(s_n\\\\\\\\), so NE set and efficient allocation are unchanged. Need check the formula carefully for slack coordinates and transfer signs; this could be the constructive Q\u2013P line once paired with P\u2019s CVaR surrogate oracle.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"12\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_36\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfor n_agents in [2,3,4,7]:\\n    alloc=[float(i+1) for i in range(n_agents)]\\n    cap=sum(alloc)\\n    price=2.0\\n    kappa=(n_agents*n_agents-n_agents+1)/(n_agents-1)**2\\n    taxes=[]\\n    repaired=[]\\n    for i in range(n_agents):\\n        other_price=(n_agents-1)*price\\n        other_alloc=cap-alloc[i]\\n        other_sigma_price=(n_agents-1)*price**2\\n        other_sigma_pa=price*other_alloc\\n        tax=(0.5*price*(price-2*other_price/(n_agents-1))\\n             -price*(alloc[i]+other_alloc-cap)/(n_agents-1)\\n             +n_agents*other_price*(alloc[i]-cap/n_agents)/(n_agents-1)**2\\n             +other_sigma_price/(2*(n_agents-1))\\n             -(other_sigma_pa-other_price*cap/n_agents)/(n_agents-1)**2)\\n        rebate=(kappa-1)*price*(other_alloc-(n_agents-1)*cap/n_agents)\\n        taxes.append(tax)\\n        repaired.append(tax+rebate)\\n        assert abs(tax-kappa*price*(alloc[i]-cap/n_agents))<1e-8\\n        assert abs(tax+rebate-price*(alloc[i]-cap/n_agents))<1e-8\\n    print(n_agents, kappa, taxes, repaired, sum(repaired))\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_36\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfor n_agents in [2,3,4,7]:\\n    alloc=[float(i+1) for i in range(n_agents)]\\n    cap=sum(alloc)\\n    price=2.0\\n    kappa=(n_agents*n_agents-n_agents+1)/(n_agents-1)**2\\n    taxes=[]\\n    repaired=[]\\n    for i in range(n_agents):\\n        other_price=(n_agents-1)*price\\n        other_alloc=cap-alloc[i]\\n        other_sigma_price=(n_agents-1)*price**2\\n        other_sigma_pa=price*other_alloc\\n        tax=(0.5*price*(price-2*other_price/(n_agents-1))\\n             -price*(alloc[i]+other_alloc-cap)/(n_agents-1)\\n             +n_agents*other_price*(alloc[i]-cap/n_agents)/(n_agents-1)**2\\n             +other_sigma_price/(2*(n_agents-1))\\n             -(other_sigma_pa-other_price*cap/n_agents)/(n_agents-1)**2)\\n        rebate=(kappa-1)*price*(other_alloc-(n_agents-1)*cap/n_agents)\\n        taxes.append(tax)\\n        repaired.append(tax+rebate)\\n        assert abs(tax-kappa*price*(alloc[i]-cap/n_agents))<1e-8\\n        assert abs(tax+rebate-price*(alloc[i]-cap/n_agents))<1e-8\\n    print(n_agents, kappa, taxes, repaired, sum(repaired))\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"2 3.0 [-3.0, 3.0] [-1.0, 1.0] 0.0\\n3 1.75 [-3.5, 0.0, 3.5] [-2.0, 0.0, 2.0] 0.0\\n4 1.4444444444444444 [-4.333333333333333, -1.4444444444444442, 1.4444444444444444, 4.333333333333333] [-3.0, -0.9999999999999998, 1.0, 3.0] 2.22